  "scope": "Memory candidate selection and SQLite dispatch-claim coordination beneath public clock/expiry/cache entry, retaining native graph observations, indexed queries, capacity counts, image codec, lease update and consuming transaction ownership.",
  "limitations": [
    "Frozen claim bodies share native query/graph/capacity, bounded codecs and storage primitives, not migrated selection/claim coordination.",
    "Memory borrows scheduling fields, reads graph readiness only for competitive candidates, and checks pressure only after finding eligible work. It preserves low-level behavior on corrupt request/counter fields rather than adding new admission.",
    "SQLite saturation reads eligibility only and does not decode candidate payloads. Idle/capacity-limited routes still commit, with original commit diagnostics.",
    "The 108 SQLite cases cover three representative operations with 36 scenarios, not all 70 durable operation combinations.",
    "Disposable corrupt fixtures restore constraints before production calls. A terminal group with a still-pending member is not an admitted checkpoint; the original SQL ignores that completed group's predecessor constraint, while memory blocks that raw terminal sequential group. No repair policy is added.",
    "Public lifecycle checks use separate handles in one process, injected update/deferred-commit failures, restart/reclaim and stale-lease rejection. They are not new concurrent races, multi-host tests, power-loss or disk-full experiments.",
    "Direct hooks model error/unwind including uncertain commits; native SQLite owns rollback. Post-commit cache adoption remains the existing outer entry's responsibility and does not revoke or retry a lease.",
    "No new Linux, remote CI, live GUI, standalone exported-source execution, full host-neutral durable recovery or throughput benchmark is claimed."
  ],
